  "prediction": "#569 B1: vsh-ff.c's lighting-unit helpers (#224's bit-exact Celsius arithmetic: ltMulCore/ltM/ltsM, ltA3, ltsA, ltR, ltMk, and ltVM/ltVA/ltDp) are rewritten, under #if __VERSION__ >= 450 (the Vulkan path), as straight-line code: loops and early returns replaced by computing every case and choosing the special ones (NaN, Inf, zero, overflow) last with mix(), and the vec3 helpers as one vector op per step. GLES and desktop GL 4.00 keep the old forms (#else). Offline: docs/lanes/litcompile569/lt_check.c transcribes old and new to C and finds 0 mismatches (ltR over all 2^32 inputs, ltMkU over its whole caller domain, the rest over specials, random words and nearby-exponent inputs), and each of 10 mutants is caught; the host Turnip harness (lane.turnipcost569) makes lit pipelines 2.5-2.7x cheaper to compile and leaves every non-lit SPIR-V byte-identical. EXPECT: NO CHANGE anywhere. MUST_NOT_MOVE, per capture and in bytes: every capture of the fixed-function lighting set -- Lighting (Two_Sided, accumulation, control, normals, range, spotlight), Material_alpha/color/color_source, Shade_model (incl. #224's nine Fixed flat colours), Specular, Specular_back, and the Fog family (Fog, Fog_gen, Fog_param, Fog_vsh, all rows since the tests do not set the lighting state themselves) -- which includes the #224 set: Lighting_range/Directional (specular 203) and Lighting_accumulation/Directional-5 (127). The rest of the full sweep (100 suites, RenderTextureLoop skipped as queue_full_sweep.sh does) compiles byte-identical SPIR-V for every shader without lighting, so it may move only by device noise. WORLD IN WHICH IT FAILS: any lit capture moves by even one byte -- then the GPU does not evaluate a helper as its C transcription does (a GLSL construct Turnip/ir3 lowers differently from the C: findMSB, mix on uint/bool, shifts of 23-31, the vector ==/!= reductions), and the suite that moves names the helper: Lighting_spotlight/_range -> ltR or ltDp on the attenuation; Specular -> ltsA/ltsM/ltR; Material_color_source -> ltVA/ltVM.",
